WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.803
Welcome to Horizon
installer training, Part 1.

2
00:00:05.253 --> 00:00:08.243
Class 2. What it means,
and how we wire now.

3
00:00:08.693 --> 00:00:11.194
By the end, you will
know what Class 2 means,

4
00:00:11.194 --> 00:00:15.166
why amps matter more than volts,
and the words we use on every job.

5
00:00:15.616 --> 00:00:18.328
This part is the base for
everything else in the course,

6
00:00:18.328 --> 00:00:19.837
so take your time with it.

7
00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:23.527
Here is the whole standard in one line.

8
00:00:23.977 --> 00:00:27.849
Every light circuit we install
can carry 4 amps. Not more.

9
00:00:28.299 --> 00:00:32.299
This part shows you why. The rest of the
course shows you how to wire it right.

10
00:00:33.649 --> 00:00:36.128
Start with the one idea that matters most.

11
00:00:36.578 --> 00:00:39.208
Volts do not start fires. Amps do.

12
00:00:39.658 --> 00:00:41.870
Volts are how hard the power pushes.

13
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.566
Amps are how much power is
actually moving through the wire.

14
00:00:46.016 --> 00:00:49.586
You need both to make power, but
it is the amps that make heat.

15
00:00:50.036 --> 00:00:53.839
Push too many amps down a
small wire, and it gets hot.

16
00:00:54.289 --> 00:00:56.315
Hot enough, and it burns.

17
00:00:56.765 --> 00:01:01.775
Our light wire is thin. It is 20 gauge,
and it comes built into the lights.

18
00:01:02.225 --> 00:01:05.877
That thin wire can only take a few
amps. Protecting it is the job.

19
00:01:07.227 --> 00:01:11.273
So how many amps can a power supply
push out, if nothing stops it?

20
00:01:11.723 --> 00:01:16.107
Look at these three bars. Each one
is a power supply we have used.

21
00:01:16.557 --> 00:01:20.722
Our first systems, Mark 1,
ran 350 watts at 12 volts.

22
00:01:20.722 --> 00:01:24.667
With nothing in the way,
that is about 29 amps.

23
00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:30.080
Mark 2 ran 600 watts at 24
volts. That is about 25 amps.

24
00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:34.124
Either way, that is far more
than thin 20 gauge wire can take.

25
00:01:34.574 --> 00:01:40.339
Mark 3 uses a 600 watt, 24 volt supply
too, but with a breaker on each circuit.

26
00:01:40.789 --> 00:01:46.298
A breaker is a switch that shuts the power
off by itself when it sees too many amps.

27
00:01:46.748 --> 00:01:49.459
Now each circuit tops out at 4 amps.

28
00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:54.722
That drop, from 25 amps down to 4,
is the whole point of the change.

29
00:01:56.072 --> 00:01:58.632
So what does Class 2 actually mean?

30
00:01:59.082 --> 00:02:01.318
Class 2 is a safety rule.

31
00:02:01.768 --> 00:02:06.801
It says a circuit can only carry a
small amount of power. About 100 watts.

32
00:02:07.251 --> 00:02:11.971
At 24 volts, 100 watts
works out to 4.16 amps.

33
00:02:12.421 --> 00:02:16.154
Keep the power that small, and
a fault cannot start a fire.

34
00:02:16.604 --> 00:02:22.090
A short or a damaged wire on a small circuit does
not have enough power behind it to start a fire.

35
00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:24.218
That is the whole idea.

36
00:02:24.668 --> 00:02:29.457
Our 4 amp circuit keeps every
run inside that small, safe size.

37
00:02:30.807 --> 00:02:33.890
Our lights are built to be
used with Class 2 power only.

38
00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.018
They expect a small circuit.

39
00:02:36.468 --> 00:02:39.783
Give them a big one, and the
safety rating means nothing.

40
00:02:40.233 --> 00:02:44.593
The lights are only as safe as the
circuit feeding them. That part is on us.

41
00:02:45.943 --> 00:02:48.434
Here are the words you
will hear on every job.

42
00:02:48.884 --> 00:02:51.224
Volt. How hard the power pushes.

43
00:02:51.674 --> 00:02:55.685
Amp. How much power flows. This
is the one that starts fires.

44
00:02:56.135 --> 00:02:58.882
Watt. Volts and amps
together. Total power.

45
00:02:59.332 --> 00:03:02.708
Breaker. A switch that shuts
the power off by itself,

46
00:03:02.708 --> 00:03:04.678
when it sees too many amps.

47
00:03:05.128 --> 00:03:08.745
Circuit. One breaker, one
wire, one group of lights.

48
00:03:09.195 --> 00:03:12.292
When you hear one circuit,
picture one breaker in the box,

49
00:03:12.292 --> 00:03:16.225
one wire leaving it, and one group
of lights at the end of that wire.

50
00:03:16.675 --> 00:03:19.758
Feeder. The wire from the
box out to the lights.

51
00:03:20.208 --> 00:03:23.941
Injection. A spot where we
feed fresh power into the run.

52
00:03:24.391 --> 00:03:29.204
So the feeder carries power from the box out
to an injection point on the run of lights.

53
00:03:29.654 --> 00:03:34.234
And P W. That is Pure White in
the app. It uses the most power.

54
00:03:35.584 --> 00:03:37.425
So why did we make this standard?

55
00:03:37.875 --> 00:03:41.440
Some of our early jobs were wired
right into the power supply.

56
00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:42.955
No breaker in between.

57
00:03:43.405 --> 00:03:47.184
That is how the industry told
us to do it. It was still wrong.

58
00:03:47.634 --> 00:03:49.695
We are going back to fix all of it.

59
00:03:50.145 --> 00:03:52.439
Nobody is in trouble for old work.

60
00:03:52.889 --> 00:03:55.668
If you open up an old job
that was wired that way,

61
00:03:55.668 --> 00:03:59.141
it is not your mistake, and
it is not a reason to copy it.

62
00:03:59.591 --> 00:04:01.687
From today, we wire this way.

63
00:04:03.037 --> 00:04:04.158
Let's lock it in.

64
00:04:04.608 --> 00:04:06.762
Amps start fires. Not volts.

65
00:04:07.212 --> 00:04:10.306
Volts push, amps flow,
and watts are the total.

66
00:04:10.756 --> 00:04:14.698
Our light wire is thin, 20
gauge. It is the part we protect.

67
00:04:15.148 --> 00:04:21.273
Class 2 means a small circuit. About
100 watts, or about 4 amps at 24 volts.

68
00:04:21.723 --> 00:04:24.854
Every circuit we install
carries 4 amps. Not more.

69
00:04:24.854 --> 00:04:27.546
And every circuit gets its own breaker.

70
00:04:27.996 --> 00:04:32.449
Next, take the Part 1 quiz. Then
in Part 2, we get into the math.
